# Copy to .env.local for development:  cp .env.example .env.local
#
# NEVER commit .env.local or any file containing real credentials.
# Deployed environments read from a managed secret store, not from files.

# ---- Application ----
APP_ENV=local                      # local | test | staging | production
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# NODE_ENV is deliberately absent. Next.js sets it itself — `development` for
# `next dev`, `production` for `next build`. Pinning it here and exporting the
# file into a shell makes `pnpm build` produce a development bundle, which then
# fails to prerender.

# ---- Database (matches docker-compose.yml) ----
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://kurdora:kurdora_local_dev@localhost:5432/kurdora?schema=public

# Scratch database Prisma Migrate diffs against. Leave empty locally and Prisma
# creates a temporary one; set it where the DB user cannot CREATE DATABASE.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=

# ---- Object storage ----
# Where the local driver writes uploaded and re-encoded images in development.
# Defaults to ./.storage (gitignored). Production requires the S3 driver
# instead; the app refuses to start without it.
LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR=

# Database integration tests (tests/db/*) run only when this is set. They create
# and drop their own throwaway databases, so point it at a LOCAL server only.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://kurdora:kurdora_local_dev@localhost:5432/kurdora_test?schema=public

# ---- Redis (cache, queues, rate limiting) ----
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ---- Auth (Phase 3) ----
# Signs access tokens (JWT, HS256). At least 32 characters.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
AUTH_SECRET=

# Encrypts TOTP secrets at rest (AES-256-GCM). EXACTLY 64 hex characters.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating this makes every enrolled authenticator unreadable — enrolled users
# would have to fall back to recovery codes and re-enrol.
AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---- Object storage (Phase 4; MinIO locally) ----
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_REGION=eu-west-2
S3_BUCKET=kurdora-local
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=kurdora
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=kurdora_local_dev

# ---- Mail (Phase 5; Mailpit locally) ----
SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
MAIL_FROM=no-reply@kurdora.local

# ---- Stripe (Phase 7 Parts 1-2 — TEST MODE ONLY) ----
#
# Use `sk_test_…` keys ONLY. A live key is REFUSED at startup: live mode is
# gated on Stripe's written approval of the platform business model, which does
# not exist (R-3, docs/15-phase-7-gate.md B-1). See src/infra/stripe/config.ts.
#
# Both are server-side only and must never be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. Leave them
# empty to run without payments — the payment routes then answer 503 rather
# than failing at the first charge.
#
# The webhook secret comes from `stripe listen`, or from the endpoint you
# registered. Without it a payment could be created that nothing can confirm,
# so a secret key without a webhook secret is refused as well.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The publishable key. NOT REQUIRED YET — leave it unset.
#
# Corrected 2026-09-13: this file previously named `STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`,
# which NOTHING in the codebase reads. The only publishable-key variable the
# schema defines is `NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` (src/infra/env.ts), and
# it is read exclusively by `clientEnv()` — which currently has no call sites,
# because there is no payment UI until Part 3.
#
# It is `NEXT_PUBLIC_` legitimately: a publishable key is public by design, and
# it is the one Stripe value allowed to reach the browser. The SECRET key and
# the WEBHOOK secret must never carry that prefix, and lint plus
# `tests/api/stripe-safety.test.ts` enforce that.
#
# Setting it today is harmless but pointless. It becomes required in Part 3.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exercising Stripe TEST MODE — the exact steps
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FULL RUNBOOK: docs/17-stripe-test-mode-setup.md — variables, dashboard setup,
# CLI commands, the webhook path, the callback URLs and the onboarding round
# trip. The summary below is kept in step with it.
#
# Phase 7 Part 2 was implemented WITHOUT Stripe credentials in the environment.
# The real-API tests (`tests/api/stripe-live.test.ts`) skip themselves, loudly,
# when no `sk_test_` key is present, and the Part 2 exit report records that
# the real Stripe API was NOT exercised. To exercise it:
#
#   1. Create a Stripe SANDBOX (test mode) account. Never a live one — a live
#      key is refused at startup regardless, because Stripe has not approved
#      Kurdora's business model in writing (R-3, docs/15-phase-7-gate.md).
#
#   2. Enable Connect in the sandbox dashboard, and set the platform profile.
#      Destination charges need the platform to be able to create accounts with
#      `controller[stripe_dashboard][type] = express`.
#
#   3. Copy the TEST keys into this file:
#        STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_…        (never sk_live_)
#        STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_…
#
#   4. Install the Stripe CLI and forward webhooks to the local endpoint:
#        stripe login
#        stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/v1/webhooks/stripe
#
#      `stripe listen` with no --events flag forwards everything, which is what
#      you want here: the handler records every event and acts only on the ones
#      in HANDLED_EVENT_TYPES (webhook-service.ts), so a narrower filter only
#      risks hiding an event you needed. Those types are currently:
#        payment_intent.succeeded, payment_intent.processing,
#        payment_intent.payment_failed, payment_intent.canceled,
#        payment_intent.requires_action, account.updated,
#        charge.succeeded, charge.updated, charge.failed
#
#      `stripe listen` prints a `whsec_…` on start. That value — NOT the one
#      from a registered dashboard endpoint — is what verifies the forwarded
#      signatures:
#        STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_…
#
#   5. Run the suites:
#        pnpm verify                 # includes the real-API tests once keyed
#        pnpm exec vitest run tests/api/stripe-live.test.ts
#
#   6. For a full onboarding round trip, a HUMAN must complete Stripe's hosted
#      form — that is the design, not a gap. No automated test can make a
#      seller payable, and neither can Kurdora: eligibility is read back from
#      the provider and nowhere else.
#
# Test cards live at https://docs.stripe.com/testing. Use 4242424242424242 for
# a success and 4000000000000002 for a decline.
